🚀 WEEK-9: ELB + Auto Scaling (Beginner Step-by-Step)

We’ll do this in 5 parts:

Create 2 EC2 servers
Install website on both
Create Load Balancer
Create Auto Scaling
Test everything
🧩 PART 1: Create EC2 Instance 1
👉 Step-by-step clicks
Open AWS Console
In search bar → type EC2 → click
👉 Amazon EC2
Left side menu → click Instances
Click “Launch Instance” (orange button top right)
🧾 Fill details
Name → webserver-1
AMI (OS)
👉 Select: Amazon Linux 2
Instance Type
👉 Select: t2.micro (free tier)
Key Pair
👉 Click:
“Create new key pair” (if not created)
Name: my-key
Download it (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔐 Security Group

Scroll down → “Network settings”

Click Edit

Add rules:

Type	Port	Source
SSH	22	My IP
HTTP	80	Anywhere (0.0.0.0/0)
🚀 Launch

Click Launch Instance

💻 Connect & Setup Server 1
Go to Instances
Select webserver-1
Click Connect
Click EC2 Instance Connect
Click Connect
👉 Run these commands
sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install httpd -y
sudo systemctl start httpd
sudo systemctl enable httpd
echo "This is Server 1" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html
🧩 PART 2: Create EC2 Instance 2

Repeat SAME steps 👇

Name → webserver-2

After connecting, run:

sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install httpd -y
sudo systemctl start httpd
sudo systemctl enable httpd
echo "This is Server 2" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html
🌐 PART 3: Create Load Balancer

We use:
👉 Application Load Balancer

🔐 Step 3.1: Create Security Group for LB
EC2 Dashboard → Left side → Security Groups
Click Create security group

Fill:

Name → lb-sg
Description → load balancer sg

Add rule:

Type	Port	Source
HTTP	80	0.0.0.0/0

Click Create

⚖️ Step 3.2: Create Load Balancer
EC2 Dashboard → Left → Load Balancers
Click Create Load Balancer

You’ll see options → choose:

👉 Application Load Balancer

Click Create

🧾 Fill details
Name → my-alb
Scheme → Internet-facing
IP → IPv4
🔊 Listener

Already there:

HTTP : 80 (keep as is)
🌍 Network Mapping
Select Default VPC
Select 2 subnets (tick any 2)
🔐 Security Group
Select → lb-sg
🎯 Step 3.3: Target Group

Click Create target group

Fill:

Name → web-servers-tg
Target type → Instance
Protocol → HTTP
Port → 80

Click Next

🧾 Register Targets
Select:
webserver-1
webserver-2

Click:
👉 Include as pending

Then:
👉 Create target group

✅ Final Step

Back to Load Balancer page:

Select created target group
Click Create Load Balancer

⏳ Wait 2–3 minutes

🌍 PART 4: Test Load Balancer
Go to → Load Balancers
Click my-alb
Copy DNS name

Example:

my-alb-xxxx.elb.amazonaws.com
Paste in browser

👉 Refresh multiple times

You should see:

"This is Server 1"
"This is Server 2"

🎉 Load balancing working!

🔁 PART 5: Auto Scaling Setup

We use:
👉 Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling

📸 Step 5.1: Create AMI
EC2 → Instances
Select webserver-1
Top menu → Actions
Click:
→ Image and templates
→ Create Image

Fill:

Name → my-webserver-AMI

Click Create

⏳ Wait → status = Available

📦 Step 5.2: Launch Template
EC2 → Left → Launch Templates
Click Create launch template

Fill:

Name → my-launch-template
AMI → select your AMI
Instance type → t2.micro
Key pair → select existing
🔐 Security Group (IMPORTANT)

⚠️ Select same SG as EC2 (allow HTTP + SSH)
👉 NOT lb-sg alone (common mistake)

Click Create

🔄 Step 5.3: Auto Scaling Group
EC2 → Left → Auto Scaling Groups
Click Create Auto Scaling Group
🧾 Fill
Name → webserver-asg
Launch Template → select yours

Click Next

🌍 Network
VPC → Default
Select ALL subnets
🔗 Attach Load Balancer
Select:
👉 Attach existing load balancer
Choose your ALB
Choose target group
❤️ Health Check
Type → HTTP
Grace period → 300
📊 Capacity
Desired → 2
Min → 1
Max → 4

Click Next → Create

🧪 PART 6: Testing Auto Scaling
🔥 Test 1: Kill Instance
Go → EC2 Instances
Select one instance
Click Terminate

👉 Wait 1–2 minutes

✅ New instance will be created automatically

🌐 Test 2: Load Balancer
Open ALB DNS again
Refresh

👉 Traffic still works
